e very  concerned about the growth of credit  card debt over time what we see is is  that the growth of credit card debt has  come about as a replacement or  substitution  of other types of debt that people used  to use so so people aren't piling on  more debt they're putting stuff on  credit cards that in the past they might  have put on a home equity line or some  other even more than that if you think  about say 30 years ago if you wanted to  buy a refrigerator or bedroom set what  would you do you go down the local  appliance store furniture store you'd  open a line of credit you pay it off 125  of dollars a month for 36 months until  it was paid off and probably paid thirty  percent intro thirty or forty percent  interest you might and into some extent  what we know is that those prices were  also embedded in the price of the price  of the goods is their competition among  credit card offers I think that anybody  who opens their mailbox knows that  there's plenty of competition for among  credit card issuers for their business  and so if somebody like Elizabeth Warren  or the Obama administration says we can  make this more competitive and less  regulated I mean it just seems silly on  the face of it's hard to imagine that um  that that market could possibly be more  competitive than it is already there are  about 6,000 issuers of credit cards in  the country right now let's talk about  mortgages because part of the cfp i  would also I mean it's about simplifying  products and and Warren and other people  actually say what we need are plain  vanilla products that everybody can  understand and is the problem in the  housing market or in the mortgage market  is it complex instruments that are that  you know the very people who are taking  these loans I can't understand mortgages  are obviously very complicated products  and a lot of people regardless of their  prime or subprime borrowers struggle  with understanding every single detail  that is in their mortgage 