ry regime  well there's a number of things they  could do without creating this whole  huge new bureaucracy number one  certainly disclosures could be improved  the Federal Trade Commission about two  years ago did an excellent study where  they were they interviewed prime and  subprime borrowers and they came up with  a whole list of ways in which in which  disclosures could be improved so that  people could shop in a better way  secondly you don't need to create a new  bureaucracy for this particular product  the Federal Trade Commission for  instance already knows how to regulate  consumer consumer credit third I think  the Republican proposal on this is  actually worth looking at which is the  Republican proposal would combine the  new elements of the cfpa with the  ability of an agency to be able to  regulate safety and soundness issues  what the Obama program does is takes  this the consumer protection mission  away from the Federal Reserve and puts  it in this one new agency but doesn't  think about what impact that might have  on on safety and soundness of financial  institute  shins final question what's in your  wallet how many credit cards are you  packing right I carry four credit cards  in my wallet and I think that's one of  the great innovations is we've gotten  rid of annual fees credit card issuers  compete for my business literally every  time I open my wallet they are competing  for my business and the ability of  consumers to hold multiple credit cards  and to choose the one that's best for  them even on a day to day basis is a  remarkable benefit to consumers and it'd  be a real shame of that if that went  away because of overzealous regulation I  want to thank Todd Sawicki of george  mason university law school and the  mercatus center for talking to us today  for reason TV i'm nick gillespie
 

